---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifeKitchenConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifeKitchenConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="ml-to-cups">
  <UnitsLifeKitchenConverter client:load mode="ml-to-cups" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Five cups, all in use</h2>
    <p>
      The cup is the least standardised unit in common use. It has no international definition, and
      several different volumes are in use, so this ml to cups page shows all of them at once.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Standard</th><th scope="col">Millilitres</th><th scope="col">Relative to US customary</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>US customary</td><td>236.588</td><td>1.000</td></tr>
        <tr><td>US legal (labelling)</td><td>240</td><td>1.014</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Metric (AU, NZ)</td><td>250</td><td>1.057</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Imperial (old UK)</td><td>284.131</td><td>1.201</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Japanese</td><td>200</td><td>0.845</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      An Australian cup in an American recipe adds nearly 6% to each wet ingredient. You cannot see that
      in a soup, but you can in a cake batter.
    </p>

    <h2>The numbers you will actually look up</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>250 mL = 1.057 US cups = exactly 1 metric cup</li>
      <li>240 mL = 1.014 US cups = exactly 1 US legal cup</li>
      <li>200 mL = 0.845 US cups</li>
      <li>500 mL = 2.11 US cups = 2 metric cups</li>
      <li>1 litre = 4.23 US cups = 4 metric cups</li>
      <li>125 mL = about half a US cup</li>
      <li>15 mL = 1 metric tablespoon</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Fluid ounces, and which one</h2>
    <p>
      A US fluid ounce is 29.5735 mL, and an imperial fluid ounce is 28.4131 mL. The imperial one is
      smaller, but the imperial pint has twenty of them and the US pint has sixteen. So an imperial
      pint (568 mL) is a fifth bigger than a US pint (473 mL). The results panel shows both figures.
    </p>

    <h2>Millilitres and grams are only equal for water</h2>
    <p>
      One millilitre of pure water weighs one gram at 4 &deg;C. That is true only for water. 250 mL of
      honey weighs 354 g, 250 mL of vegetable oil weighs 227 g, and 250 mL of flour weighs about 130 g.
      Pick an ingredient in the tool, and it gives the weight as well as the volume.
    </p>

    <h2>Measuring liquid accurately</h2>
    <p>
      Use a jug on a level surface, read at eye level, and read the bottom of the meniscus (the curved
      surface a liquid makes against glass). Dry measuring cups are for filling to the brim and
      levelling. Liquid jugs have the graduation below the rim, so you can carry them. If you use one
      for the other, the error can be several percent.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
